## sn1.35
sn1.35:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthi in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn1.35:1.2 | S: Then, late at night, several glorious deities of the host of the fault-finders, lighting up the entire Jeta’s Grove, went up to the Buddha, and stood in the air. | T: Then, when the night had advanced, a number of “faultfinding” devatās, of stunning beauty, illuminating the entire Jeta’s Grove, approached the Blessed One and stood in the air.
sn1.35:1.3 | S: Standing in the air, one deity spoke this verse in the Buddha’s presence: | T: Then one devatā, standing in the air, recited this verse in the presence of the Blessed One:
sn1.35:2.1 | S: “Someone who pretends | T: “If one shows oneself in one way
sn1.35:2.2 | S: to be other than they really are, | T: While actually being otherwise, What one enjoys is obtained by theft
sn1.35:2.3 | S: is like a cheating gambler | T: Like the gains of a cheating gambler.”
sn1.35:2.4 | S: who enjoys what was gained by theft. | T: (none)
sn1.35:3.1 | S: You should only say what you would do; | T: Another devatā: “One should speak as one would act;
sn1.35:3.2 | S: you shouldn’t say what you wouldn’t do. | T: Don’t speak as one wouldn’t act.
sn1.35:3.3 | S: The wise will recognize | T: The wise clearly discern the person
sn1.35:3.4 | S: one who talks without doing.” | T: Who does not practise what he preaches.”
sn1.35:4.1 | S: “Not just by speaking, | T: The Blessed One: “Not by mere speech nor solely by listening
sn1.35:4.2 | S: nor solely by listening, | T: (none)
sn1.35:4.3 | S: are you able to progress | T: Can one advance on this firm path of practice
sn1.35:4.4 | S: on this hard path, | T: (none)
sn1.35:4.5 | S: by which the attentive practicing absorption | T: By which the wise ones, the meditators,
sn1.35:4.6 | S: are released from Māra’s bonds. | T: Are released from the bondage of Mara.
sn1.35:5.1 | S: The attentive certainly don’t act like that, | T: “Truly, the wise do not pretend,
sn1.35:5.2 | S: for they understand the way of the world. | T: For they have understood the way of the world.
sn1.35:5.3 | S: The attentive are quenched by understanding, | T: By final knowledge the wise are quenched:
sn1.35:5.4 | S: they’ve crossed over clinging to the world.” | T: They have crossed over attachment to the world.”
sn1.35:6.1 | S: Then those deities landed on the ground, bowed with their heads to the Buddha’s feet and said, | T: Then those devatās, having alighted on the earth, prostrated themselves with their heads at the Blessed One’s feet and said to the Blessed One:
sn1.35:6.2 | S: “We made a mistake, sir. It was foolish, stupid, and unskillful of us to presume to attack the Buddha! | T: “A transgression overcame us, venerable sir, being so foolish, so stupid, so unskilful that we imagined we could assail the Blessed One.
sn1.35:6.3 | S: Please, sir, accept our mistake for what it is, so we will restrain ourselves in future.” | T: Let the Blessed One pardon us for our transgression seen as such for the sake of restraint in the future.”
sn1.35:6.4 | S: At that, the Buddha smiled. | T: Then the Blessed One displayed a smile.
sn1.35:6.5 | S: Then those deities, becoming even more fault-finding, flew up in the air. | T: Those devatās, finding fault to an even greater extent, then rose up into the air.
sn1.35:6.6 | S: One deity spoke this verse in the Buddha’s presence: | T: One devatā recited this verse in the presence of the Blessed One:
sn1.35:7.1 | S: “If you don’t give your pardon | T: “If one does not grant pardon
sn1.35:7.2 | S: when a mistake is confessed, | T: To those who confess transgression,
sn1.35:7.3 | S: with hidden anger and heavy hate, | T: Angry at heart, intent on hate,
sn1.35:7.4 | S: you’re stuck in your enmity.” | T: One strongly harbours enmity.”
sn1.35:8.1 | S: “Suppose no mistake were found, | T: The Blessed One: “If there was no transgression,
sn1.35:8.2 | S: and no one here had gone astray: | T: If here there was no going astray,
sn1.35:8.3 | S: if enmities were not settled, | T: And if enmities were appeased,
sn1.35:8.4 | S: how could that be skillful?” | T: Then one would be faultless here.”
sn1.35:9.1 | S: “Who makes no mistakes? | T: A devatā: “For whom are there no transgressions?
sn1.35:9.2 | S: Who doesn’t go astray? | T: For whom is there no going astray?
sn1.35:9.3 | S: Who doesn’t fall into confusion? | T: Who has not fallen into confusion?
sn1.35:9.4 | S: Who is attentive, ever mindful?” | T: And who is the wise one, ever mindful?”
sn1.35:10.1 | S: “The Realized One, the Buddha, | T: The Blessed One: “The Tathagata, the Enlightened One,
sn1.35:10.2 | S: sympathetic for all beings: | T: Full of compassion for all beings:
sn1.35:10.3 | S: that’s who makes no mistakes, | T: For him there are no transgressions,
sn1.35:10.4 | S: and that’s who doesn’t go astray. | T: For him there is no going astray;
sn1.35:10.5 | S: He doesn’t fall into confusion, | T: He has not fallen into confusion,
sn1.35:10.6 | S: for he’s attentive, ever mindful. | T: And he is the wise one, ever mindful.
sn1.35:11.1 | S: If you don’t give your pardon | T: “If one does not grant pardon
sn1.35:11.2 | S: when a mistake is confessed, | T: To those who confess transgression,
sn1.35:11.3 | S: with hidden anger and heavy hate, | T: Angry at heart, intent on hate,
sn1.35:11.4 | S: you’re stuck in your enmity. | T: One strongly harbours enmity.
sn1.35:11.5 | S: I don’t approve of such enmity, | T: In that enmity I do not delight,
sn1.35:11.6 | S: and so I pardon your mistake.” | T: Thus I pardon your transgression.”

## sn1.81
sn1.81:1.1 | S: “Who in the world is free of strife? | T: “Who here in the world are placid?
sn1.81:1.2 | S: Whose life is not wasted? | T: Whose mode of life is not squandered?
sn1.81:1.3 | S: Who here completely understands desire? | T: Who here fully understand desire?
sn1.81:1.4 | S: Who always lives as their own master? | T: Who enjoy perpetual freedom?
sn1.81:2.1 | S: To whom do mother, father, and brothers | T: “Whom do parents and brothers worship
sn1.81:2.2 | S: bow when they’re established? | T: When he stands firmly established?
sn1.81:2.3 | S: Who here, though low born, | T: Who is the one of humble birth
sn1.81:2.4 | S: is bowed to even by aristocrats?” | T: That even khattiyas here salute?”
sn1.81:3.1 | S: “Ascetics are free of strife in the world. | T: “Ascetics are placid in the world;
sn1.81:3.2 | S: The life of ascetics is not wasted. | T: The ascetic life is not squandered;
sn1.81:3.3 | S: Ascetics completely understand desire. | T: Ascetics fully understand desire;
sn1.81:3.4 | S: Ascetics always live as their own master. | T: They enjoy perpetual freedom.
sn1.81:4.1 | S: Mother, father, and brothers | T: “Parents and brothers worship an ascetic
sn1.81:4.2 | S: bow to ascetics when they’re established. | T: When he stands firmly established.
sn1.81:4.3 | S: Even though an ascetic is low born, | T: Though an ascetic be of humble birth
sn1.81:4.4 | S: they’re bowed to even by aristocrats.” | T: Even khattiyas here salute him.”
sn1.81:4.5 | P: Chetvāvaggo aṭṭhamo. | T: (none)
sn1.81:5.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn1.81:5.1 | P: Chetvā rathañca cittañca, | T: (none)
sn1.81:5.2 | P: vuṭṭhi bhītā najīrati; | T: (none)
sn1.81:5.3 | P: Issaraṁ kāmaṁ pātheyyaṁ, | T: (none)
sn1.81:5.4 | P: pajjoto araṇena cāti. | T: (none)
