## sn11.23
sn11.23:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn11.23:1.2 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn11.23:1.3 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, Vepacitti, lord of titans, was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, was sick, afflicted, gravely ill.
sn11.23:1.4 | S: So Sakka went to see him to ask after his illness. | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, approached Vepacitti to inquire about his illness.
sn11.23:1.5 | S: Vepacitti saw Sakka coming off in the distance, | T: Vepacitti saw Sakka coming in the distance and said to him:
sn11.23:1.6 | S: and said to him, | T: (none)
sn11.23:1.7 | S: ‘Heal me, lord of gods!’ | T: ‘Cure me, lord of the devas.’—
sn11.23:1.8 | S: ‘Teach me, Vepacitti, the Sambari sorcery.’ | T: ‘Teach me, Vepacitti, the Sambari magic.’—
sn11.23:1.9 | S: ‘I can’t do that, good fellow, until I have consulted with the titans.’ | T: ‘I won’t teach it, dear sir, until I have asked the asuras for permission.’
sn11.23:1.10 | S: Then Vepacitti, lord of titans, asked the titans, | T: “Then, bhikkhus, Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, asked the asuras:
sn11.23:1.11 | S: ‘Good fellows, may I teach the Sambari sorcery to Sakka, lord of gods?’ | T: ‘May I teach the Sambari magic to Sakka, lord of the devas?’—
sn11.23:1.12 | S: ‘Do not, good fellow, teach the Sambari sorcery to Sakka!’ | T: ‘Do not teach him the Sambari magic, dear sir.’
sn11.23:1.13 | S: So Vepacitti addressed Sakka in verse: | T: “Then, bhikkhus, Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, addressed Sakka, lord of the devas, in verse:
sn11.23:2.1 | S: ‘O Maghavā, O Sakka, | T: “‘A magician—O Maghava, Sakka,
sn11.23:2.2 | S: king of gods, Sujā’s husband: | T: King of devas, Suja’s husband—
sn11.23:2.3 | S: a sorceror like Sambara falls | T: Goes to the terrible hell, Like Sambara,
sn11.23:2.4 | S: into the terrible hell for a full century.’” | T: for a hundred years.’”

## sn12.58
sn12.58:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.58:1.2 | S: “There are things that tighten the fetters. When you concentrate on the gratification provided by these things, name and form are conceived. | T: “Bhikkhus, when one dwells contemplating gratification in things that can fetter, there is a descent of name-and-form.
sn12.58:1.3 | S: Name and form are requirements for the six sense fields. … | T: With name-and-form as condition, the six sense bases come to be….
sn12.58:1.4 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.58:2.1 | S: Suppose there was a great tree. | T: “Suppose, bhikkhus, there was a great tree,
sn12.58:2.2 | S: And its roots going downwards and across all draw the sap upwards. | T: and all its roots going downwards and across would send the sap upwards.
sn12.58:2.3 | S: Fed and fuelled by that, the great tree would stand for a long time. | T: Sustained by that sap, nourished by it, that great tree would stand for a very long time.
sn12.58:2.4 | S: In the same way, there are things that tighten the fetters. When you concentrate on the gratification provided by these things, name and form are conceived. … | T: So too, when one lives contemplating gratification in things that can fetter, there is a descent of name-and-form…. Such is the origin of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.58:3.1 | S: There are things that tighten the fetters. When you concentrate on the drawbacks of these things, name and form are not conceived. | T: “When, bhikkhus, one dwells contemplating danger in things that can fetter, there is no descent of name-and-form.
sn12.58:3.2 | S: When name and form cease, the six sense fields cease. … | T: With the cessation of name-and-form comes cessation of the six sense bases….
sn12.58:3.3 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases. | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.
sn12.58:4.1 | S: Suppose there was a great tree. | T: “Suppose, bhikkhus, there was a great tree.
sn12.58:4.2 | S: Then a person comes along with a spade and basket. … | T: Then a man would come along bringing a shovel and a basket.
sn12.58:4.3 | S: In this way the great tree is cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future. | T: He would cut down the tree at its foot … he would winnow the ashes in a strong wind or let them be carried away by the swift current of a river. Thus that great tree would be cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated so that it is no more subject to future arising.
sn12.58:4.4 | S: In the same way, there are things that tighten the fetters. When you concentrate on the drawbacks of these things, name and form are not conceived. | T: “So too, bhikkhus, when one dwells contemplating danger in things that can fetter, there is no descent of name-and-form….
sn12.58:4.5 | S: When name and form cease, the six sense fields cease. … | T: (none)
sn12.58:4.6 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.” | T: Such is the cessation of this whole mass of suffering.”
sn12.58:4.7 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.230
sn35.230:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose an angler was to cast a baited hook into a deep lake. | T: “Bhikkhus, suppose a fisherman would cast a baited hook into a deep lake,
sn35.230:1.2 | S: Seeing the bait, a fish would swallow it. | T: and a fish on the lookout for food would swallow it.
sn35.230:1.3 | S: And so the fish that swallowed the hook would meet with tragedy and disaster, and the angler can do what he wants with it. | T: That fish who has thus swallowed the fisherman’s hook would meet with calamity and disaster, and the fisherman could do with it as he wishes.
sn35.230:2.1 | S: In the same way, there are these six hooks in the world that mean tragedy and slaughter for living creatures. | T: So too, bhikkhus, there are these six hooks in the world for the calamity of beings, for the slaughter of living beings.
sn35.230:2.2 | S: What six? | T: (none)
sn35.230:2.3 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.230:2.4 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu seeks delight in them, welcomes them, and remains holding to them,
sn35.230:2.5 | S: they’re called a mendicant who has swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One can do with them what he wants. | T: he is called a bhikkhu who has swallowed Mara’s hook. He has met with calamity and disaster, and the Evil One can do with him as he wishes.
sn35.230:2.6 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are, bhikkhus, sounds cognizable by the ear …
sn35.230:3.1 | S: ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable … tantalizing.
sn35.230:3.2 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a bhikkhu seeks delight in them …
sn35.230:3.3 | S: they’re called a mendicant who has swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One can do with them what he wants. | T: the Evil One can do with him as he wishes.
sn35.230:4.1 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.230:4.2 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, they’re called a mendicant who hasn’t swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve broken the hook, destroyed it. They haven’t met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One cannot do with them what he wants. | T: If a bhikkhu does not seek delight in them, does not welcome them, and does not remain holding to them, he is called a bhikkhu who has not swallowed Mara’s hook, who has broken the hook, demolished the hook. He has not met with calamity and disaster, and the Evil One cannot do with him as he wishes.
sn35.230:5.1 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are, bhikkhus, sounds cognizable by the ear …
sn35.230:5.2 | S: ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable … tantalizing.
sn35.230:5.3 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, they’re called a mendicant who hasn’t swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve broken the hook, destroyed it. They haven’t met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One cannot do with them what he wants.” | T: If a bhikkhu does not seek delight in them … the Evil One cannot do with him as he wishes.”
sn35.230:5.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.30
sn35.30:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the practice fit for uprooting all conceiving. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the way that is appropriate for uprooting all conceivings.
sn35.30:1.2 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak. … | T: Listen to that and attend closely, I will speak….
sn35.30:1.3 | S: And what is the practice fit for uprooting all conceiving? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the way that is appropriate for uprooting all conceivings?
sn35.30:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant does not conceive anything to be the eye, does not conceive it in the eye, does not conceive it as the eye, and does not conceive that ‘the eye is mine.’ | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu does not conceive the eye, does not conceive in the eye, does not conceive from the eye, does not conceive, ‘The eye is mine.’
sn35.30:1.5 | S: They don’t conceive anything to be sights, they don’t conceive it in sights, they don’t conceive it as sights, and they don’t conceive that ‘sights are mine.’ | T: He does not conceive forms …

