## sn4.20
sn4.20:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Kosalans, in a wilderness hut on the slopes of the Himalayas. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Kosalans in a small forest hut in the Himalayan region.
sn4.20:1.2 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, when the Blessed One was alone in seclusion, a reflection arose in his mind thus:
sn4.20:1.3 | S: “I wonder if it’s possible to rule legitimately, without killing or having someone kill for you; without conquering or having someone conquer for you; without sorrowing or causing sorrow?” | T: “Is it possible to exercise rulership righteously: without killing and without instigating others to kill, without confiscating and without instigating others to confiscate, without sorrowing and without causing sorrow?”
sn4.20:2.1 | S: And then Māra the Wicked, knowing the Buddha’s train of thought, went up to him and said, | T: Then Mara the Evil One, having known with his own mind the reflection in the Blessed One’s mind, approached the Blessed One and said to him:
sn4.20:2.2 | S: “Rule, Blessed One! Rule, Holy One! Rule legitimately, without killing or having someone kill for you; without conquering or having someone conquer for you; without sorrowing or causing sorrow!” | T: “Venerable sir, let the Blessed One exercise rulership righteously: without killing and without instigating others to kill, without confiscating and without instigating others to confiscate, without sorrowing and without instigating others to cause sorrow.”
sn4.20:2.3 | S: “But what do you see, Wicked One, that you say this to me?” | T: “But what do you see, Evil One, that you speak thus to me?”
sn4.20:2.4 | P: ‘kāretu, bhante, bhagavā rajjaṁ, kāretu sugato, rajjaṁ ahanaṁ aghātayaṁ ajinaṁ ajāpayaṁ asocaṁ asocāpayaṁ dhammenā’”ti? | T: (none)
sn4.20:2.5 | S: “The Blessed One, sir, has developed and cultivated the four bases for psychic power, made them a vehicle and a basis, kept them up, consolidated them, and properly implemented them. | T: “Venerable sir, the Blessed One has developed and cultivated the four bases for spiritual power, made them a vehicle, made them a basis, stabilized them, exercised himself in them, and fully perfected them.
sn4.20:2.6 | S: If he wished, the Blessed One need only determine that the Himalaya, king of mountains, was gold, and it would turn into gold.” | T: And, venerable sir, if the Blessed One wishes, he need only resolve that the Himalayas, the king of mountains, should become gold, and it would turn to gold.”
sn4.20:3.1 | S: “Take a golden mountain, | T: The Blessed One: “If there were a mountain made of gold,
sn4.20:3.2 | S: entirely of native gold, and double it— | T: Made entirely of solid gold,
sn4.20:3.3 | S: it’s still not enough for one! | T: Not double this would suffice for one:
sn4.20:3.4 | S: Knowing this, live a moral life. | T: Having known this, fare evenly.
sn4.20:4.1 | S: When a personage has seen where suffering comes from | T: (none)
sn4.20:4.2 | S: how could they incline toward sensual pleasures? | T: “How could a person incline to sensual pleasures Who has seen the source whence suffering springs?
sn4.20:4.3 | S: Realizing that attachment is a chain in the world, | T: Having known acquisition as a tie in the world,
sn4.20:4.4 | S: a personage would train to remove it.” | T: A person should train for its removal.”
sn4.20:5.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The Buddha knows me! The Holy One knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The Blessed One knows me, the Fortunate One knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.
sn4.20:5.2 | P: Dutiyo vaggo. | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.1 | P: Pāsāṇo sīho sakalikaṁ, | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.2 | P: Patirūpañca mānasaṁ; | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.3 | P: Pattaṁ āyatanaṁ piṇḍaṁ, | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.4 | P: Kassakaṁ rajjena te dasāti. | T: (none)

## sn47.95-104
sn47.95-104:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are five higher fetters. | T: (To be elaborated parallel to SN45.171.) Higher Fetters “Bhikkhus, there are these five higher fetters.
sn47.95-104:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
sn47.95-104:1.3 | S: Desire for rebirth in the realm of luminous form, desire for rebirth in the formless realm, conceit, restlessness, and ignorance. | T: Lust for form, lust for the formless, conceit, restlessness, ignorance.
sn47.95-104:1.4 | S: These are the five higher fetters. | T: These are the five higher fetters.
sn47.95-104:1.5 | S: The four kinds of mindfulness meditation should be developed for the direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing, and giving up of these five higher fetters. | T: The four establishments of mindfulness are to be developed for direct knowledge of these five higher fetters, for the full understanding of them, for their utter destruction, for their abandoning.
sn47.95-104:2.1 | S: What four? | T: “What four?
sn47.95-104:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.95-104:2.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings …
sn47.95-104:2.4 | S: mind … | T: mind in mind …
sn47.95-104:2.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn47.95-104:2.6 | S: These four kinds of mindfulness meditation should be developed for the direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing, and giving up of these five higher fetters.” | T: These four establishments of mindfulness are to be developed for direct knowledge of these five higher fetters, for the full understanding of them, for their utter destruction, for their abandoning.”
sn47.95-104:2.7 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn47.95-104:3.1 | S: (The Linked Discourses on Mindfulness Meditation should be told in full as in the Linked Discourses on the Path. These ten discourses correspond to SN 45.171–179, with the above as the final discourse.) | T: (none)
sn47.95-104:3.2 | P: Oghavaggo dasamo. | T: (none)
sn47.95-104:4.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn47.95-104:4.1 | S: Floods, yokes, grasping, | T: Floods, bonds, kinds of clinging,
sn47.95-104:4.2 | S: ties, and underlying tendencies, | T: Knots, and underlying tendencies,
sn47.95-104:4.3 | S: kinds of sensual stimulation, hindrances, | T: Cords of sensual pleasure, hindrances,
sn47.95-104:4.4 | S: aggregates, and fetters high and low. | T: Aggregates, fetters lower and higher.
sn47.95-104:4.5 | S: The Linked Discourses on Mindfulness Meditation, the third section. | T: The Connected Discourses on the Establishments of Mindfulness is to be elaborated in the same way as the Connected Discourses on the Path.

## an8.23
an8.23:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Āḷavī, at Āḷavī’s premier shrine. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Āḷavī at the Aggāḷava Shrine.
an8.23:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus….
an8.23:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, you should remember the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī as someone who has seven amazing and incredible qualities. | T: “Bhikkhus, you should remember Hatthaka of Āḷavī as one who possesses seven astounding and amazing qualities.
an8.23:1.4 | S: What seven? | T: What seven?
an8.23:1.5 | S: He’s faithful, | T: (1) Hatthaka of Āḷavī is endowed with faith.
an8.23:1.6 | S: ethical, | T: (2) He is virtuous,
an8.23:1.7 | S: conscientious, | T: and (3) has a sense of moral shame and
an8.23:1.8 | S: prudent, | T: (4) moral dread.
an8.23:1.9 | S: learned, | T: (5) He is learned,
an8.23:1.10 | S: generous, | T: (6) generous,
an8.23:1.11 | S: and wise. | T: and (7) wise.
an8.23:1.12 | S: You should remember the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī as someone who possesses these seven amazing and incredible qualities.” | T: You should remember Hatthaka of Āḷavī as one who possesses these seven astounding and amazing qualities.”
an8.23:1.13 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
an8.23:1.14 | S: When he had spoken, the Holy One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling. | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling.
an8.23:2.1 | S: Then a certain mendicant robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went to the home of the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī, where he sat down on the seat spread out. | T: Then, in the morning, a certain bhikkhu dressed, took his bowl and robe, and went to the residence of Hatthaka of Āḷavī. When he arrived, he sat down on the seat that was prepared for him.
an8.23:2.2 | S: Then Hatthaka went up to that mendicant, bowed, and sat down to one side. The mendicant said to Hatthaka: | T: Then Hatthaka of Āḷavī approached the bhikkhu, paid homage to him, and sat down to one side. The bhikkhu then said to him:
an8.23:3.1 | S: “Respectable sir, the Buddha declared that you have seven amazing and incredible qualities. | T: “Friend, the Blessed One declared that you possess seven astounding and amazing qualities.
an8.23:3.2 | S: What seven? | T: What seven?
an8.23:3.3 | S: He said that you’re faithful, | T: ‘Bhikkhus, Hatthaka of Āḷavī is endowed with faith.
an8.23:3.4 | S: ethical, | T: He is virtuous and has a sense of moral shame and moral dread.
an8.23:3.5 | S: conscientious, | T: (none)
an8.23:3.6 | S: prudent, | T: (none)
an8.23:3.7 | S: learned, | T: He is learned,
an8.23:3.8 | S: generous, | T: generous,
an8.23:3.9 | S: and wise. | T: and wise.’
an8.23:3.10 | S: The Buddha declared that you possess these seven amazing and incredible qualities.” | T: The Blessed One declared that you possess these seven astounding and amazing qualities.”
an8.23:3.11 | S: “But sir, I trust that no white-clothed lay people were present?” | T: “I hope, Bhante, that no white-robed layman was present?”
an8.23:3.12 | S: “No, there weren’t any white-clothed lay people present.” | T: “No, friend. No white-robed layman was present.”
an8.23:3.13 | S: “That’s good, sir.” | T: “That’s good, Bhante.”
an8.23:4.1 | S: Then that mendicant, after taking almsfood in Hatthaka of Āḷavī’s home, rose from his seat and left. | T: Then that bhikkhu, having received almsfood at the residence of Hatthaka of Āḷavī, rose from his seat and departed.
an8.23:4.2 | S: Then after the meal, on his return from almsround, he went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him of what he had discussed with the householder Hatthaka. The Buddha said: | T: After his meal, on returning from his alms round, he approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and reported to him all that had happened. The Blessed One said:
an8.23:5.1 | P: “Idhāhaṁ, bhante, pubbaṇhasamayaṁ nivāsetvā pattacīvaramādāya yena hatthakassa āḷavakassa nivesanaṁ tenupasaṅkamiṁ; upasaṅkamitvā paññatte āsane nisīdiṁ. | T: (none)
an8.23:5.2 | P: Atha kho, bhante, hatthako āḷavako yenāhaṁ tenupasaṅkami; upasaṅkamitvā maṁ abhivādetvā ekamantaṁ nisīdi. | T: (none)
an8.23:5.3 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinnaṁ kho ahaṁ, bhante, hatthakaṁ āḷavakaṁ etadavacaṁ: | T: (none)
an8.23:5.4 | P: ‘sattahi kho tvaṁ, āvuso, acchariyehi abbhutehi dhammehi samannāgato bhagavatā byākato. | T: (none)
an8.23:5.5 | P: Katamehi sattahi? | T: (none)
an8.23:5.6 | P: Saddho, bhikkhave, hatthako āḷavako; | T: (none)
an8.23:5.7 | P: sīlavā …pe… | T: (none)
an8.23:5.8 | P: hirīmā … | T: (none)
an8.23:5.9 | P: ottappī … | T: (none)
an8.23:5.10 | P: bahussuto … | T: (none)
an8.23:5.11 | P: cāgavā … | T: (none)
an8.23:5.12 | P: paññavā, bhikkhave, hatthako āḷavakoti. | T: (none)
an8.23:5.13 | P: Imehi kho tvaṁ, āvuso, sattahi acchariyehi abbhutehi dhammehi samannāgato bhagavatā byākato’ti. | T: (none)
an8.23:6.1 | P: Evaṁ vutte, bhante, hatthako maṁ etadavoca: | T: (none)
an8.23:6.2 | P: ‘kaccittha, bhante, na koci gihī ahosi odātavasano’ti? | T: (none)
an8.23:6.3 | P: ‘Na hettha, āvuso, koci gihī ahosi odātavasano’ti. | T: (none)
an8.23:6.4 | P: ‘Sādhu, bhante, yadettha na koci gihī ahosi odātavasano’”ti. | T: (none)
an8.23:7.1 | S: “Good, good, mendicant! | T: “Good, good, bhikkhu!
an8.23:7.2 | S: That gentleman has few wishes. | T: That clansman has few desires,
an8.23:7.3 | S: He doesn’t want his own good qualities to be made known to others. | T: since he does not want his inner wholesome qualities to be known by others.
an8.23:7.4 | S: Well then, mendicant, you should remember the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī as someone who has this eighth amazing and incredible quality, that is, fewness of wishes.” | T: Therefore, bhikkhu, you should remember Hatthaka of Āḷavī as one who possesses this eighth astounding and amazing quality, that is, (8) fewness of desires.”
an8.23:7.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.28
an10.28:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Kajaṅgalā in a bamboo grove. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Kajaṅgalā in the Bamboo Grove.
an10.28:1.2 | S: Then several lay followers of Kajaṅgalā went to the nun Kajaṅgalikā, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to her: | T: Then a number of lay followers from Kajaṅgalā approached the bhikkhunī from Kajaṅgalā, paid homage to her, sat down to one side, and said to her:
an10.28:2.1 | S: “Ma’am, this was said by the Buddha in ‘The Great Questions’: | T: “Noble lady, this was said by the Blessed One in ‘The Great Questions’:
an10.28:2.2 | S: ‘One thing: question, summary recital, and answer. Two … Three … Four … Five … Six … Seven … Eight … Nine … Ten things: question, summary recital, and answer.’ | T: ‘A question about one, a concise statement about one, an explanation of one. A question about two, a concise statement about two, an explanation of two. A question about three, a concise statement about three, an explanation of three. A question about four, a concise statement about four, an explanation of four. A question about five, a concise statement about five, an explanation of five. A question about six, a concise statement about six, an explanation of six. A question about seven, a concise statement about seven, an explanation of seven. A question about eight, a concise statement about eight, an explanation of eight. A question about nine, a concise statement about nine, an explanation of nine. A question about ten, a concise statement about ten, an explanation of ten.’
an10.28:2.3 | S: How should we see the detailed meaning of the Buddha’s brief statement?” | T: How, noble lady, is the meaning of this statement that the Blessed One spoke in brief to be seen in detail?”
an10.28:3.1 | S: “Respectable sirs, I haven’t heard and learned this in the presence of the Buddha or from esteemed mendicants. | T: “Friends, I have not heard and learned this in the presence of the Blessed One, nor have I heard and learned this in the presence of esteemed bhikkhus.
an10.28:3.2 | S: But as to how it seems to me, | T: However, listen and attend closely as I explain what it seems to mean to me.”
an10.28:3.3 | S: listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: (none)
an10.28:3.4 | S: “Yes, ma’am,” replied the lay followers. | T: “Yes, noble lady,” those lay followers of Kajaṅgalā replied.
an10.28:3.5 | S: The nun Kajaṅgalikā said this: | T: The bhikkhunī of Kajaṅgalā said this:
an10.28:4.1 | S: “‘One thing: question, summary recital, and answer.’ That’s what the Buddha said, | T: (1) “When it was said by the Blessed One: ‘A question about one, a concise statement about one, an explanation of one,’ with reference to what was this said?
an10.28:4.2 | S: but why did he say it? | T: (none)
an10.28:4.3 | S: Becoming completely disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed regarding one thing, seeing its limits and fully comprehending its meaning, a mendicant makes an end of suffering in this very life. | T: When a bhikkhu is completely disenchanted with one thing, completely dispassionate toward it, completely liberated from it, completely sees its delimitations, and completely breaks through its meaning, in this very life he makes an end of suffering.
an10.28:4.4 | S: What one thing? | T: What one thing?
an10.28:4.5 | S: ‘All sentient beings are sustained by food.’ | T: All beings exist through nutriment.
an10.28:4.6 | S: Becoming completely disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed regarding this one thing, seeing its limits and fully comprehending its meaning, a mendicant makes an end of suffering in this very life. | T: When a bhikkhu is completely disenchanted with this one thing, completely dispassionate toward it, completely liberated from it, completely sees its delimitations, and completely breaks through its meaning, in this very life he makes an end of suffering.
an10.28:4.7 | S: ‘One thing: question, summary recital, and answer.’ That’s what the Buddha said, and this is why he said it. | T: “When it was said by the Blessed One: ‘A question about one, a concise statement about one, an explanation of one,’ it is with reference to this that this was said.
an10.28:5.1 | P: ‘Dve pañhā dve uddesā dve veyyākaraṇānī’ti iti, kho panetaṁ vuttaṁ bhagavatā. | T: (2) “When it was said by the Blessed One: ‘A question about two, a concise statement about two, an explanation of two,’
an10.28:5.2 | P: Kiñcetaṁ paṭicca vuttaṁ? | T: with reference to what was this said?
an10.28:5.3 | P: Dvīsu, āvuso, dhammesu bhikkhu sammā nibbindamāno sammā virajjamāno sammā vimuccamāno sammā pariyantadassāvī sammadatthaṁ abhisamecca diṭṭheva dhamme dukkhassantakaro hoti. | T: When a bhikkhu is completely disenchanted with two things, completely dispassionate toward them, completely liberated from them, completely sees their delimitations, and completely breaks through their meaning, in this very life he makes an end of suffering.
an10.28:5.4 | S: What two? | T: What two things?
